{"target":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a0fb34218038167fbf464ab2ef2e97f182cc1244","text":"Retired: this is the abandoned ancestor of a lane that never stopped moving, not a dropped task.\n\nWHY I CHECKED BEFORE RETIRING RATHER THAN AFTER. A reneged row with a withdrawn promise is the exact shape of work that quietly dies, and the standing rule here is that a procedural failure never implies the task should not be completed. So the question was not whether the bookkeeping looked bad — it did — but whether the substance had a live home. It does.\n\nTHE EVIDENCE. This request sits at sequence 10894. The decision-records web UI lane runs continuously past it for more than two thousand positions, through real implementation heads and real independent reviews: 3fd75543, 6e29fa99, 47763758, f6489c78, 93f0b2a6 and e1798050, with review rounds at 7592621d, 5f32e2ca, ffcbb5ab, d73a78d1, 96d6f7dd and 01cec162, several of them satisfied. The feature this row asked for was built, reviewed repeatedly, and is still being repaired against reviewer verdicts today under afe72492 and 3a4cc296, both reported and both with claude. Nothing about the goal is being abandoned by closing this row; the goal is what those rows are for.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT CLAIMING. Unlike the chess row I retired beside this one, I am not saying this work is in main. Those heads are candidates under review, and the two live children are answering changes-requested verdicts. The claim is narrower and it is the one that matters for retirement: the obligation is carried by live successor rows, so this row adds a second, staler copy of an assignment claude already holds rather than holding anything of its own.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY WENT WRONG HERE, since it is the pattern hugh asked about. The lane was reassigned from ox-builder, my first refile 2d03a803 was stale from birth, this second refile was promised and then the promise was withdrawn — and the work carried on under later rows that rest on other bases. Every step was individually reasonable and the net effect is an open row over work somebody else is already doing. The row to watch is never the one that looks abandoned; it is the one whose successors nobody wired back to it.\n\nIts three dependents are all dead — the retired promise 5adc75a8, a stale assert of mine dffb3494, and one act. No document cites it; I grepped docs, notes and the root markdown before retiring."}